Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
When the disciples came to Jesus, they asked him, saying, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of the heavens?"
Jesus called a little child to Him, and set him in the midst of them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is the truth. You must change and become like little children. If you do not, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes like this child in humility will be the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever accepts me, will accept me with this little child in my name.
But if one of these little ones who believe in me is led astray, it would be better for him to have a great millstone fastened around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
Woe to the world because of the things that cause people to sin! And those things must come, but woe to the person who causes one of these things to come!
If your hand or foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter life maimed than to have two hands or feet and be thrown into eternal fire, 'where their worm does not die and the fire is not quenched.'
If your right eye causes you to stumble, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with only one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. So also the one who is lost, like a lost sheep.
Look to it that you do not despise one of these little ones. For I tell you that their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father who is in heaven.
Translation
What do you think? Let's suppose that a man owns a hundred sheep, and that one of them strays from him. Should he not leave the ninety-nine on the mountains and go in search of the one that strays?
What I am going to tell you now is a fact. He is more pleased with the one that has not gone astray from among the other 99.
And he is like a father to you in heaven. For he does not want anyone of these little ones to perish. When one of them wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go and rebuke him, between you and him, alone with each other. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother.
But what if he will not listen to you? Then take one or two more, so that by the mouth of two or three witnesses every fact may be confirmed. (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But if he refuses to listen even to you, then take him for the church. And if he refuses to listen even from the church, then let him be to you as a Gentile or a tax collector.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. What you close on earth will be closed in heaven. And what you open on earth will be opened in heaven.
And I tell you again: If two of you agree on earth about anything they ask, it will be done for them by my Father in heaven. 'Let it be to you according to your faith.' Amen.
Where two or three are gathered in my name, there I am with them." The servant who did not have compassion
Peter came to Jesus and said, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The parable of the Kingdom of the Heavens is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his slaves regarding the money that each one of them had.
And the King began indeed, when a man who owed millions of dollars in debts was brought to him.
The man could not pay, so his master ordered that the man, his wife, his children, and all that he possessed be sold to pay the debt.
The slave fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay back everything."
The master, having compassion on his servant, forgave him the debt and set him free.
The slave who owned the money went out and found his companion who was owed a few dinars. He took him by the neck, saying, "Pay what you owe me."
And the other servant fell on his knees. And he said, pleading, "Give me a chance, my master. I will pay you back the amount."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went, arrested the man, and imprisoned him. Until he is released, he is required to pay off his debt.
The other servants saw what happened. It greatly disturbed them. So they went and told their master everything that had occurred.
His master called the first servant and said to him, "You are just like another wicked servant! All that debt I forgave you because you repented to me."
Did you not want to be in his favor, having an advantage over the enemies, just as you saved me from the enemy?
His master was angry and handed him over to the guards. He will be punished until he recovers everything he owes.
Thus, my heavenly Father will also do to each of you, unless you forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus taught about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan.
Large crowds followed him, and he healed many sick people there.
Some Pharisees came to Him, testing Him, and saying, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife and they shall be one flesh. (Genesis 2:24)
Two shall become one flesh; so then they are no longer two, but one flesh. What therefore God has joined together, let not man tear asunder.
They said: "Why then did Moses command that a man should repudiate his wife with a writing and send her away from him?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not like that from the beginning."
This is what I am telling you. Whoever divorces his wife and marries another is an adulterer. A man may divorce his wife if she is only unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If this is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone who hears my words and believes will have eternal life. Only those who are born of God will have eternal life. These are the ones who are born not by human will, but by the will of God. It is by this will that all who believe in God's Son are born into God's kingdom. And everyone who has this hope in him purifies himself, just as Christ is pure."
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. And some, people made them that way. And others, they made themselves that way in order to serve the kingdom of heaven. Whoever can accept living like this, let them do so." Little children are brought to Jesus.
Some people brought little children to Jesus, and they asked him to let the children come to him and to pray for them. But the disciples rebuked the people.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus put his hands on them, then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man said to him, "O teacher, what good thing must I do to inherit eternal life?"
Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is one who is good. That one is God. If you want to enter into life, then keep the commandments."
What then? the man asked. Jesus said, "You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not give false testimony."
And honor your father and your mother. (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) And "you shall love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
I have obeyed all those commandments, said the young man. So what else am I to do?
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the boy heard that, he left sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to the disciples: "Truly I tell you, it is difficult for a rich person to enter the kingdom of the heavens."
And I say to you again: It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were utterly amazed and said, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "This is impossible for people, but with God all things are possible."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything and followed you! So what will be our reward?"
Jesus said to them, "What I am about to tell you is true; when all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. And all of you who have followed me will also sit on glorious thrones, and you will rule over the twelve tribes of Israel."
Whoever gives up a home, or parents, or children, or a field, or a business, will have it made for them for my sake and for the sake of the Gospel. And they will be given a hundred times more and will inherit eternal life.
But many of the first will be last, and many of the last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The parable of the kingdom of heaven is like a man, a householder, who planted a vineyard in his garden. He built a watchtower and entrusted
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
He went out again at about nine o'clock in the morning. He saw others standing in the market doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also, go and work in my vineyard. I will pay you the right wage."
So they went. He also went out near noon on that day at the third hour and did the same thing.
He went out around five o'clock and found others standing there. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long without working?"
They said, "Because no one has hired us." And he said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard."
And when evening came, the landowner spoke with the manager of the workers, saying, "Call the workers so they may receive their pay. Begin with the last ones hired, and then go to the first ones hired."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived. Each of them received a full day's wage as usual.
When the first group's workers came, he rented them at the same wage rate as he had rented the others.
When they received it, they started complaining about its owner.
"We were informed," they said, "that these newly appointed individuals have only worked for one hour, yet you have paid them the same amount you pay us. We are the ones who have done most of the work, spending the whole day under the scorching sun."
The shopkeeper said to someone, "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for your usual day's wage?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give those I newly appointed the same salary I gave you.
Don't I have the right to do what I want with my money?! Do you feel wronged because I gave generously to others?!
The middle becomes first, and the first becomes middle.
Jesus went up to Jerusalem, and on the way he took the twelve aside and began to tell them.
We went up to Jerusalem, he said... and the Son of Man will be delivered to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death.
Then they will hand him over to some non-Jewish people, and they will mock him and flog him, and they will crucify him, and on the third day he will rise from the dead!
The mother of my sons Zebedee came to Jesus with her two sons, and she fell down before him on her knees, asking him for a request.
"What is it you want?" Jesus asked her. And she said, "Grant me that one of my sons may sit at your right hand in your kingdom, and the other at your left hand."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking." Can you drink the cup that I drink, or be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with?" They said to him, "Yes."
Jesus said to them, "Assuredly, you will drink from the cup that I drink from. And you will be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with. But to sit at my right hand or at my left hand is not mine to give; these places are prepared for those who are prepared for them."
The other ten pupils heard about this; they were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus gathered them and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over their people, and their great men exercise authority over them. The rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and those who are great exercise authority over them.
Do not be like this. Rather, whoever wants to be first among you shall be your servant.
And whoever wants to be the first, let him be your servant.
Be like the Son of Man. For he did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many, so they might be freed from their slavery.
Jesus and his disciples leave Jericho, and a large crowd follows him.
And two blind men sitting by the road. And when they heard that Jesus passed by them, they cried out saying: "Have mercy on us, O Lord, O Son of David!"
The crowd told them to be quiet. They told them to be silent. But the men cried out even more loudly, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stood and called them, saying, "What do you want Me to do for you?"
Our lord, we hope to see.
Jesus, finding in his heart a great compassion, touched their eyes, and immediately their sight followed. So they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
While they were approaching Jerusalem and had reached the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples with instructions:
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. And as soon as you enter it, you will find a colt bound there, which no one has ever ridden. And its mother is there with it. Loose them both, and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say that the master needs them. He will send them immediately.
This happened so that what was spoken by the prophets might be fulfilled: 'That it was necessary for this to happen in this way.'
Say to the daughter of Zion, Behold, your king is coming to you, gentle, and riding on an donkey, on a colt, the daughter of a donkey. (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had instructed them to do.
They brought a donkey and a colt. They put their clothes on them. Jesus sat on the clothes.
A forgiving multitude in the midst of the road spread their coats. And the rest cut the boughs from the trees and spread them on the road.
Some of the people went before him, and the others followed him, crying out: " أوصنا لابن داود! مبارك الآتي باسم الرب ". " أوصنا في الأعالي ". (مزمور ١١٨:٢٦)
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up, saying, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus the prophet, from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus cleansed the temple
Jesus entered the temple and began to drive out all those who were selling and buying in it. And he overturned the tables of the moneychangers and the seats of those who sold doves.
He said to them: "It is written: 'My house shall be called a house of prayer,'" (Isaiah 56:7) and "You have made it a den of robbers." (Jeremiah 7:11)
He came to him in the temple, a blind man and paralytics, and he healed them.
And the chief priests and the scribes of the law saw the wonderful things that he was doing. And they saw the children in the temple, crying out, "Hosanna in the highest for the Son of David!" And they were filled with anger when they saw this.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. Jesus said, "Yes. Have you never read, 'Out of the mouth of infants and nursing babies you have prepared praise'?" (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the crowd and went away from there to the house, and a crowd followed him. And he went in and
Early in the morning, Jesus was going back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He came to it and found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "Never shall anything be done for you again, until all things shall have been restored." And immediately the fig tree withered away to the very sight.
The disciples were amazed, saying, "How can the fig tree dry up so quickly!"
Jesus answered, "Truly, I tell you, if you have faith and do not doubt, you will not only do what has been done to the fig tree, but even if you tell this mountain, 'Go, throw yourself into the sea,' it will be done.
"If you believe, God will answer your prayers." Defining the authority of Jesus.
Jesus entered the temple courts while he was teaching, and the chief priests and the scribes stood by him, and they said to him, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will also ask you one question, and if you tell me the answer, then I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
Where was John's baptism? From heaven or from men? They discussed among themselves saying, 'If we say, From heaven, he will say to us, Why then did you not believe him?'
But what if we say: "Of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered him, "We do not know." Jesus said to them, "I also will not tell you by what authority I do these things."
What do you think about this? There was a man who had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go and work in the vineyard today."
No, I won't, the son answered. But he changed his mind afterwards and went.
Then the father went to the other son and said the same words to him. The son replied, "I will, Father." But he did not.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" They said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly I say to you, that the tax collectors and the prostitutes go into the kingdom of God before you.
The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man who owned a piece of land planted a vineyard around it with a fence. He dug a well for the vintage, and built a press as well. Then he turned the vineyard over to cultivators who worked for him, and set out on a journey.
And when the harvest was near, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect his share of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. And they killed another. And they threw the third to the ground with stones until he died.
Then the man sent other slaves to the farmers. And this time he sent larger numbers than the first time. And the farmers treated them likewise.
Finally, he sent them his son. And he said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will inherit all of our master's possessions someday. Let's kill him, and everything will be ours."
So they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard, and then they killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do to those tenants?
"Those evil people will be destroyed." They answered. "Then the vineyard will be rented to others, who will give him his share of the fruit at the times of the harvest.
Jesus said to them, "Have you not even read this from the Scriptures: 'The stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone; this is the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118:22, 23)"
Here I am telling you: The kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a people who will produce its fruit.
Everyone who falls on that stone will be shattered. But the stone will crush whoever falls on it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. And they realized that he was speaking about them.
So they sought a way to seize him, but they were afraid of the crowd because the people were considering him a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus also spoke to them in other parables.
This is what the kingdom of heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
His servants were sent to those who were invited to the dinner, and the servants ordered them to come, but they refused.
Then he sent also other messengers, saying, "Tell those who are invited, 'Behold, I have prepared my dinner; I have killed my oxen and my fatlings all are ready. Come to the marriage feast.' "
But the people paid no attention. And one went off to his field and another to his business.
The rest seized his servants. They mistreated them and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to eliminate them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to the servants, "The dinner is ready for the feast. But the invited guests whom I invited were not worthy to attend."
Go out into the street corners. Invite to dinner everyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets and gathered everyone they found, both good and bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man who was not wearing ceremonial clothes.
My friend, he asked him, "How did you get in here without a wedding garment?" The man could not say anything.
The king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet. And throw him into the outer darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and began to plot how they might seize Jesus by what he said.
They sent their disciples to him. They sent the Herodians with them. And they said, "Teacher, we know that you are a truthful man, and you teach the way of God in truth, and you do not care for anyone, nor do you regard the person of anyone."
Say to us then, what do you see? Is it permissible for us to give the Jizya to Caesar or not?
But Jesus perceived their malice, and said, "Hypocrites! Why do you seek to arrest me?"
Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes. So they brought him a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
They said, "To Caesar." And He said to them, "Give to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
They were amazed at this, so they departed from it and went away. Marriage after the resurrection.
On the same day, Sadducees came to Jesus and asked him a question. For they did not believe that people would rise from the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses commanded us: If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry his widow and have children for him, so that his brother's name will not be forgotten."
There were seven brothers among us. The first one of us got married and died. And since he had no children, he bequeathed his wife to his brother.
And the same thing happened to the second and third brothers. And this was repeated until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Who would his wife be then among them? She was married to all seven of them."
Jesus answered, "You have sinned because you are ignorant of the Scriptures and do not know the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry. Nor will their parents give them in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
As for the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what God has said to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. And I am the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) God is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living.
The multitude that heard it were astonished at what he taught them. His greatest commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus, so the Pharisees met together.
A certain expert in the law stood up to test Jesus with a question.
He asked him, "Teacher, which commandment is the most deserving to be called the greatest among the commandments in the law?"
Jesus answered: "Love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind." (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and great commandment.
And the second, similar one: "You shall love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
All the writers and the prophets lean on these two commandments." Who is the son of the Messiah?
The Pharisees met together, and Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he? They answered, "The son of David."
He said to them: "Then, why does David call him Lord? Because the Holy Spirit was speaking of David himself. David said:
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at My right hand, until I make Your enemies Your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
If they call him Lord, how can he be his son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him again.
Jesus rules over the Pharisees and the scribes of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and his disciples.
He said, "The scribes and the Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
Do not obey them, lest the greatness of their actions be laid bare. Rather, do everything that they command you to do, but do not follow their actions, for they do not follow what they forbid people from doing.
They tie heavy loads and place them on people's shoulders. And they themselves do not want to raise a finger to move.
All that they do, they do it so that people may see them. And upon their foreheads and their arms they place small boxes containing verses from the Holy Book. And they make these boxes very broad. Also, they lengthen the tassels on their coats to a great degree.
They love to recline upon thrones at banquets. And to be distinguished in assemblies.
They like to wander between the shops. They are pleased to call out: "My Lord."
But do not be called teachers, for one is your teacher and all of you are brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth your father. For you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called a teacher. You have one teacher, the Christ.
He among you who is the greatest must become the servant of all.
He who exalts himself shall be abased; and he who abases himself shall be exalted.
But woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you shut the kingdom of heaven in the face of men. For you neither enter into the kingdom of heaven, nor allow those to enter who are going to it.
Translation
Woe to you, you scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! For you are like a sea and a land to make one convert, a proselyte like you, and you make him a son of hell, double more than yourselves.
Woe to you, you blind guides! For if anyone says, 'In the name of the Temple,' it is not binding. But whoever swears by the gold of the Temple, he is bound by his oath.
Are you blind and ignorant? What is the most important thing? Gold? Or the structure that makes gold holy?
You also say: "If someone swears by the altar, it is not binding upon him; but whoever swears by the offering that is his obligation, he is bound thereby."
Oh, my blind one! What is more important? Is it the gift or the altar that sanctifies the gift?
Whoever swears by the altar, let him swear by it and by all that is upon it.
And whoever swears by the name of the temple of the Lord has sworn by it and by the one who dwells in it.
And whoever swears by the heavens has indeed sworn by the throne of Allah and whoever is upon it.
But woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! because you tithe the mint and the cumin and every herb, and neglect justice and the love of God. These you ought to have done, without leaving the others undone.
You are the leaders of misguidance! You sift the bread of your sustenance from the very small specks of very fine dross, but you swallow whole camels!
Woe to you, you scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but are full of decay and corruption on the inside.
Blind Jew! Clean first the inside of the glass and the bowl. Then the outside will also appear clean.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which outwardly appear beautiful, but within are full of the bones of the dead and of all uncleanliness.
In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. Thus are you, from within, empty of all that is wrong, and you appear to the world as kindness and righteousness from without. You pretend to be what you are not.
Woe to you, you scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! For you build the tombs of the prophets and you decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And you say, "If only we had been given the Scripture, we would have been more guided than they." Then We sent you (O Muhammad) upon a religion of the past
You testify against yourselves, acknowledging that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
So complete what remains of your religious duties and fulfill your oaths to God. These are the limits set by God, and those who obey God and His Messenger will be admitted to gardens where streams flow, and they will be given beautiful dwellings as a reward for their deeds.
Oh nest of toxic hornets! How will you escape being condemned to the fire?
Therefore I am sending you prophets and wise men and teachers. Some of them you will kill and some of them you will crucify, and others you will flog in your synagogues. And you will pursue them from city to city.
You will pay the price for every drop of righteous blood that has been shed on earth, from the blood of my righteous brother Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berekiah. Zechariah whom you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am about to relate to you is a reality. All this will happen to those who are living now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You are killing the prophets and stoning those who are sent to you. How often I wanted to gather you like a hen gathers her brood under her wings, but you were not willing!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, 'Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.' 118:26
Signs of the Hour
Jesus left the temple, and as he was walking away, his disciples approached him to show him the structure of the temple.
Do you see all these things? Jesus asked. What I am about to tell you is true. Not one stone will be left here on another; all will be thrown down.
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives, and only His disciples were with Him. And they said to Him, "Tell us, when will these things be? And what is the sign that precedes Your coming, and the coming of the end of the world?"
Jesus answered: "Awake and beware, lest you become a heretic!"
Many will come in my name and say, 'I am the Messiah!' and they will lead many astray.
You will hear about wars. You will also hear people talking about wars of the future. Do not be alarmed. These things must happen. But the end is not yet.
One nation will fight against another nation, and one kingdom will fight against another kingdom, and the people will suffer from hunger, and there will be earthquakes in many places.
These are the beginning of labor pains.
At that time, people will deliver you over to be persecuted, and they will kill you, and you will be hated by all the nations because of my name.
At that time many will fall away from the faith and will hate one another. And many false prophets will arise and lead many astray. And because of the increase of wickedness, the love of most will grow cold.
Many false prophets will appear. They will lead many people astray.
Because evil will prevail, the love of most people will grow cold.
But the one who endures to the end, he will be saved.
This gospel of the kingdom will be preached in all the world as a witness to all the nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel will speak of "the detestable thing that causes desolation." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) And someday, you will see it standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to get anything.
No one in the field should have to come back to get his coat off of it.
Woe to those who bear burdens, from what they bear! Woe to those who give suck their children, from what they nurse them.
Ask that you not run away in the cold or on a Saturday.
There will be great torment in those days, worse than all that has come before from the beginning of the world until now. And there will be nothing like it ever again.
If time were not shortened, no one would survive alive. But it will be shortened because of the chosen ones whom God has chosen.
Then one of them will say to you: Behold, Christ is here or there! Do not believe it.
False messiahs and deceptive false Christs will appear. They will perform great signs and wonders, and they will deceive, if possible, even the elect.
Look, I already told you.
