"Be quiet!" said Jesus firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit shook the man violently, then burst out of him with a terrified scream.
All the people were amazed and said to one another, "What is this? A new teaching - and with authority! He commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey him!"
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the synagogue. They immediately entered the home of Simon, Peter, James, and John.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed, suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, and helped her rise. The fever left her, and she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, there came to Jesus all the sick, those possessed by demons, the paralyzed, the lame, the blind, and all those who were afflicted in any way.
All the people in town gathered at the door.
Jesus healed many of them, who were sick with various diseases. He also drove out many demons, but He did not allow the demons to speak because they knew who He was. Jesus' prayer in a quiet place.
It was still very early and the darkness was deep. Jesus arose and went out of the house. He went to a place where He could be alone. And there He prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they shouted, saying, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to another place, so that I can stay in the nearby towns also. For this purpose I have come out."
He traveled throughout Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and driving out demons. He healed many who suffered from various diseases; among them was a man who was severely leprous.
A man came to Jesus who had a skin disease. He fell on his knees, pleading with him, saying, "If you want to, you can make me clean."
Jesus found within himself great compassion. He stretched out his hand, touched him, and said to him, "I will, be clean!"
He was immediately cured and the disease departed, returning him to a state of well-being.
Jesus immediately banished him and sternly warned the man.
"Do not tell this to anyone," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded as testimony for the priest and the people that you are pure."
But the man went out and began to proclaim the matter, and spread the news to everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter a city openly, but remained outside in desolate places. Yet people would come to him from everywhere.
Jesus Heals a Paralyzed Man
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned to his house.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. There was no more space even outside the door. So Jesus was preaching the word of God to them.
Four people brought a man who cannot walk, carrying him.
But they could not get near Jesus because of the crowd. So they went up on the roof, where Jesus was, and lowered the man on his mat to him in front of Jesus.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the scribes were sitting there. They are thinking,
Why does this man speak like this? He is saying something very evil! Forgiveness of sins is only for God
Jesus first ate that they are thinking about their things, so he said to them: "What matters are you thinking about?"
Is it to be said to this man, "Your sins are forgiven you"? Or to say, "Rise and take up your bed and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then he told the sick, "Get up, pick up your mat, and walk."
He said to them: "I tell you, get up. Carry your mat and go to your home."
And he rose up and chose for himself a great company, and then he went on, with them watching him. And everyone was astonished and marveled, and they said, "What is this? A feast for everyone! How can this be?" And as for Jesus, he said to them, "The healthy do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance."
And once again Jesus went out to the shore of the Sea of Galilee; and a great crowd gathered around him, and he taught them.
And he was walking, when he was looking at Lazzerus son of Alpheus, and Lazzerus was sitting at the place of the tax collection. And Jesus said to him: "Follow me". And he arose and followed him.
Jesus was later reclining at the table in the house of Levi, and with him were tax collectors and many sinners who were eating with him, because a large crowd was following him.
There were some scribes of the Pharisees. When they saw Jesus eating with tax collectors and sinners, they said to his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this, and he said to them, "It is not those who are well who need a physician, but those who are ill. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners."
The disciples of John and of the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus and said, "John's disciples are fasting, and the disciples of the Pharisees are also fasting. But your disciples are eating."
Jesus answered, "How can the guests fast while the bridegroom is still with them? As long as the bridegroom is with them, they cannot fast.
But the time will come when the bridegroom is taken from them. On that day they go away fasting.
People do not patch old worn-out clothes with new pieces of fabric. If they did, the new piece would tear away from the old one, and the rips would get even worse.
People do not put new wine into old bottles. If they do, the new wine will burst the bottles, the wine will spill out, and the bottles will be ruined. Instead, they put new wine into new bottles.» Jesus, Lord of the Sabbath, as well.
On the Sabbath, Jesus was walking in the field with his disciples, and his disciples began to pick the grain and were grinding it with their hands.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, if he does this on the Sabbath, it is against the law. Why do your disciples do what is not lawful?"
He answered them, "Have you never read what David did? He and his men were hungry, in need of food."
This was in the days of Abiathar the high priest. And David went into the house of God and ate the bread of the presence, which it was not lawful to eat but for the priests only, and David also gave it to his young men.
Then Jesus said to them: 'The Sabbath was made for man's sake, and not man for the Sabbath's sake.
So the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath.
Healing on Saturday.
And at that time Jesus also entered the synagogue, and there was a man there whose hand was withered, shriveled up.
And some of the Pharisees were watching him to find a reason to complain about him. They were waiting to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man with the withered, crooked hand: "Rise and stand in the center." And He said to him, "Stretch out your hand."
Then Jesus said to them: "What is the truth that should guide our actions on the Sabbath? Is it right to do good or evil? Should we save a life or take it?" They remained silent.
Jesus looked around him with anger, because he was very affected by the hardness of their hearts. Then he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was restored as if it were new.
So the Pharisees went away and conspired with the Herodians against Jesus, to put him to death.
Jesus set out with his disciples for the Sea of Galilee, and a great crowd from Galilee followed him.
The people heard about all that Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and from Idumea and from the regions east of the Jordan, and also from the areas around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This was to keep the crowd away from him.
Jesus healed many people, so the sick were pushing to touch him.
And when people who were filled with evil spirits saw Jesus, they fell down before him, and the spirits cried out, saying, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus instructed them not to disclose his identity. Jesus's selection of the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up on a mountain and called to him people, so they came to him.
He appointed twelve of them as disciples and named them apostles. From that time on, they kept company with him. He also sent them out to preach.
They will have the power to cast out demons.
Jesus chose the twelve disciples, and Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus surnamed them Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, and Philip, and Bartholomew, and Matthew, and Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were also Thaddaeus, and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He is the one who will later deliver Jesus to his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered the house, and a crowd also gathered around Him, so that He could hardly find time to eat with His disciples.
His family heard about it. They came to take him. They said, "He has gone mad."
There were some Pharisees who had come down from Jerusalem. They said, "By the prince of demons he casts out demons." "By the prince of demons he casts out demons."
Jesus called them and said to them a parable: "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If the kingdom itself fights, it will not survive.
If the family splits, it will not hold.
